### Books REST API - Chapter 12 (Backend Security)
### Use VS Code "REST Client" or import into Postman.

@host  = http://localhost:8000
@token = paste-the-access_token-here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Public and security headers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

### Health
GET {{host}}/

### List books - inspect response headers for security headers
GET {{host}}/api/books

### Show one book
GET {{host}}/api/books/1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

### Register
POST {{host}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name":     "Secure Reader",
  "email":    "secure-reader@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

### Login as the seeded admin
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email":    "admin@books.test",
  "password": "password"
}

### Login as the seeded member
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email":    "member@books.test",
  "password": "password"
}

### Wrong password - run 6 times quickly, expect 429 on the 6th request
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "admin@books.test", "password": "wrong" }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Protected calls - copy access_token above into @token
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

### Current user
GET {{host}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Create a book - created_by is stamped from the JWT
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "title":  "Refactoring",
  "author": "Martin Fowler",
  "year":   2018,
  "genre":  "Software Engineering"
}

### Validation check - expect 400 because the year is in the future
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{ "title": "Future Book", "author": "X", "year": 9999 }

### XSS-safe JSON output - then call GET /api/books and inspect escaped output
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "title":  "<script>alert(1)</script>",
  "author": "Security Test",
  "year":   2024,
  "genre":  "Testing"
}

### IDOR check - member updating book 1 should get 403, admin should get 200
PUT {{host}}/api/books/1
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "year":  2009,
  "genre": "Software Craftsmanship"
}

### Delete a book - admin only
DELETE {{host}}/api/books/2
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. CORS allow-list checks
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

### Disallowed origin
OPTIONS {{host}}/api/books
Origin: http://attacker.example
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST

### Allowed origin
OPTIONS {{host}}/api/books
Origin: http://localhost:5173
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Audit log database check
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

### Run in MySQL after tests:
### SELECT occurred_at, action, target, ip_address FROM audit_log ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 10;
